":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","line":19,"producer_hash_method":"atlas-whitespace-normalized-text","producer_statement_sha256":"0c28c0725eca032e2a32f31f9656431fbabd132b64ad6d8044df01f0134b4c0c","provenance":{"kind":"computed","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","source":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex"},"sha256":"sha256:3fc023f89a5f541517848bf449fac09b36b79cf4bb3ad6a6732528a3e9bf2a86","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex#L19"},"statement_kind":"theorem","text":"\\label{thm:main}\nThere is a finite connected three-dimensional CW complex $X$ such that\n\\[\n H_i(\\widetilde X;\\Z)=0\\quad(i>2),\\qquad\n H^3(X;M)=0\\quad\\text{for every }\\Z[\\pi_1(X)]\\text{-module }M,\n\\]\nbut $X$ is not homotopy equivalent to any finite CW complex of\ndimension at most two."}
